<h2>The contract and the claims</h2>
<p>DMRC awarded the appellant a contract in 2008, valued at Rs 33,76,81,216.12, for architectural finishing works at seven elevated stations on the Inderlok–Mundka line of Delhi MRTS Phase-II. Disputes over delay, measurement and the final bill culminated in DMRC terminating the contract on 4 June 2010. In arbitration, the contractor raised 32 claims; the award of 28 September 2018 allowed 7, partly allowed 3 and rejected 22. The contractor&rsquo;s Section 34 petition was dismissed on 4 May 2023, and the matter reached the Division Bench under Section 37 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996.</p>

<h2>The objections that went unanswered</h2>
<p>The appellant&rsquo;s Section 34 petition had raised specific, claim-wise grounds: that termination was effected without the two-day further notice required by clauses 11.4.2(b), (g) and (i) of the General Conditions of Contract; that the performance bank guarantee was forfeited without proof of loss; that extra-item and false-ceiling claims were wrongly rejected; that liquidated damages were wrongly recovered; and that costs under Sections 31(8) and 31A were wrongly denied. The Single Judge&rsquo;s judgment, the Bench found, dealt with none of these in substance — it reproduced the award&rsquo;s reasoning and declared the objections consistent with the contract.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the judgment:</strong> &ldquo;the specific contentions raised by the appellant under Section 34 of the Act were not considered&rdquo; — and &ldquo;the failure to consider the specific objections raised by the appellant violates the principles of natural justice.&rdquo;</p>
</div>

<h2>Why remand, not decision</h2>
<p>The Bench was careful about institutional boundaries. A Section 37 court reviews whether the Section 34 court stayed within its statutory limits; it is a review of a review, not a third innings on merits. Where the Section 34 court never actually adjudicated the objections, there is no decision whose conformity with Section 34 can be tested. The only course consistent with the scheme — drawn from the line of authority including <em>NHAI v. M. Hakeem</em>, <em>Fiza Developers</em>, <em>HUDA v. Mehta Construction</em> and <em>Bombay Slum Redevelopment Corporation v. Samir Narain Bhojwani</em> — was to remit the objections for fresh consideration, expressly leaving all contentions open.</p>

<h2>The anatomy of a proper Section 34 adjudication</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. Identify each ground.</strong> Patent illegality, conflict with public policy, natural justice violations — each objection must be identified as pleaded, claim by claim.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. Engage with the material.</strong> The court must test the objection against the award&rsquo;s reasoning and the contractual clauses invoked — not substitute a global impression of plausibility.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. Record reasons on each.</strong> A conclusion that the award is a &ldquo;plausible view&rdquo; is the end point of analysis, not a replacement for it.</div>
</div>

<h2>What both sides should take away</h2>
<p>For award debtors, the decision is a reminder that the narrowness of Section 34 is not a licence for summary disposal: the right to have objections actually considered is part of the process due, and its denial is itself a ground of appeal. For award holders, the sobering arithmetic: an award of September 2018 now returns to the Section 34 queue in late 2026, with enforcement correspondingly deferred. Parties defending awards should therefore assist the Section 34 court to deal with each objection expressly — a judgment that answers every ground is the fastest road to finality. The decision will be of particular interest to contractors and public-sector employers litigating GCC-based terminations, where notice clauses and guarantee forfeitures recur as grounds of challenge.</p>
